## Design system
Document or link the canonical source for:
- typography;
- spacing/layout grid;
- color and semantic tokens;
- elevation/borders/radii;
- iconography/imagery;
- motion;
- component states and variants.

Prefer reusable tokens/components over one-off styling. A design-system change should update the canonical primitive before duplicating local exceptions.

## Required UI states
For every meaningful user journey, define the applicable states:
- initial/loading/skeleton;
- empty/no-results;
- success;
- validation/help;
- partial/degraded;
- offline/retry;
- permission/authentication denied;
- error/recovery;
- destructive confirmation/undo where applicable.

## Accessibility
For web products, WCAG 2.2 Level AA is the default target unless the product contract sets a stricter or legally required standard.

Verify as applicable:
- semantic structure and accessible names;
- keyboard operation and visible, unobscured focus;
- contrast and non-color cues;
- target size and pointer alternatives;
- labels, validation, errors and accessible authentication;
- reduced motion and animation safety;
- screen-reader announcements for dynamic state.

Automated accessibility checks are useful but do not replace keyboard/screen-reader or task-level review for important journeys.

